  "readmeFile": "README.md",
  "releaseNotes": "AutoMapperLite 4.4.0 — current official release; all prior versions are deprecated.\n\n\t\t\tMapping engine and performance redesign. Each source/destination type pair now\n\t\t\tcompiles into a cached delegate via System.Linq.Expressions on first use, instead of\n\t\t\tinterpreting mappings through reflection. Dispatch, collection element resolution, and\n\t\t\tnested same-name property mapping are all resolved once and cached rather than\n\t\t\tre-resolved per call. See docs/performance.html for full measured results.\n\n\t\t\t• Added: IMapper.Map<TSource, TDestination>(TSource source) — a strongly-typed\n\t\t\t  overload for call sites where the source type is known at compile time. This is the\n\t\t\t  fastest way to call AutoMapperLite, and now also works directly for collections:\n\t\t\t  mapper.Map<List<TSourceItem>, List<TDestItem>>(sourceList) skips resolving\n\t\t\t  the source collection's runtime type.\n\t\t\t• Added: collection destinations are no longer limited to List<T>.\n\t\t\t  Map<TDestination>(object) now also accepts T[], IList<T>, ICollection<T>,\n\t\t\t  IReadOnlyList<T>, IReadOnlyCollection<T>, and IEnumerable<T>. The same\n\t\t\t  flexibility applies to same-name nested collection properties.\n\t\t\t• Performance: fixed a collection-loop interface-dispatch cost that was limiting\n\t\t\t  collection-mapping throughput — casting the source to IEnumerable<T> and using\n\t\t\t  foreach forced List<T>'s own struct enumerator to be boxed through the interface on\n\t\t\t  every call. The loop now indexes List<T>/arrays directly. Measured: a 100-item\n\t\t\t  collection dropped from ~1.3 us to ~0.78 us (object API) / ~0.67 us (generic API); a\n\t\t\t  5,000-item collection dropped from ~60-74 us to ~35 us. AutoMapperLite now beats\n\t\t\t  AutoMapper on collection mapping at every size from 1,000 items upward (previously it\n\t\t\t  trailed), and at 100,000 items is within 8% of hand-written manual mapping's own time.\n\t\t\t• Behavior change (disclosed): exceptions raised during mapping now propagate directly\n\t\t\t  as InvalidOperationException instead of being wrapped in TargetInvocationException.\n\t\t\t• Behavior change (disclosed): List<T> mapping resolves the item map from the source\n\t\t\t  collection's declared item type rather than each element's runtime type.\n\t\t\t• Allocations for simple, medium, nested, and deeply-nested object mapping now match\n\t\t\t  hand-written manual mapping exactly. Collection mapping allocation now matches manual\n\t\t\t  mapping/Mapster exactly via the generic API, from 10 items to 100,000.\n\t\t\t• No public API breaking changes since 4.0.2 (only for anyone implementing IMapper\n\t\t\t  directly rather than using the built-in Mapper class, due to the new interface\n\t\t\t  member above).\n\n\t\t\tPrevious (4.0.2):\n\t\t\t• Fixed: updated test tooling required net8.0+; the test project now targets\n\t\t\t  net8.0-net10.0 (the library itself still targets net6.0-net10.0).\n\t\t\t• Fixed: Activator.CreateInstance failures now throw a clear InvalidOperationException\n\t\t\t  naming the type, instead of a bare MissingMethodException.\n\t\t\t• Added real, measured BenchmarkDotNet comparisons against manual mapping, AutoMapper,\n\t\t\t  and Mapster.\n\t\t\t• Verified and documented: null source properties are skipped (not assigned), auto-map\n\t\t\t  requires an exact type match, and records with init-only properties map correctly.",
